---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';

const title = 'How to schedule a meeting across three time zones';
const description =
  'A repeatable, fair process for selecting and documenting a meeting time across three time zones.';
---

<Base title={title} description={description} path="/guides/three-time-zone-meetings">
  <article class="max-w-2xl">
    <nav aria-label="Breadcrumb" class="mb-5 text-xs text-paper-500">
      <a href="/guides" class="ring-focus hover:text-paper-900">Guides</a>
      <span aria-hidden="true" class="mx-1.5">/</span>
      <span aria-current="page">Three time zones</span>
    </nav>
    <header class="mb-8">
      <p class="text-[11px] font-semibold tracking-[0.15em] text-paper-500 uppercase">
        Team planning
      </p>
      <h1 class="mt-2 text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-balance sm:text-4xl">
        How to schedule a meeting across three time zones
      </h1>
      <p class="mt-4 text-lg leading-relaxed text-paper-700">
        With three locations, there may be no perfect overlap. The goal is not to discover a magic
        hour; it is to make the trade-off clear, repeatable and fair.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section class="mt-8">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">1. Use cities, not abbreviations</h2>
      <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-paper-700">
        Ask each person for their city or IANA time zone, not only “PST”, “IST” or “CST”. Those
        abbreviations can be ambiguous and often hide daylight-saving changes. A city gives the
        calendar enough information to resolve the right local time for each future date.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-8">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">2. Write down real availability</h2>
      <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-paper-700">
        A nominal 09:00–17:00 day is only a starting point. One person may start early for school
        pickup, another may accept one evening a week, and a third may have a protected lunch. Put
        those limits into the <a
          href="/overlap"
          class="ring-focus rounded font-medium text-paper-900 underline underline-offset-2 hover:text-paper-700"
          >meeting planner</a
        > before looking for the overlap.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-8">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">3. Decide what “fair” means</h2>
      <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-paper-700">
        Fair can mean the least total inconvenience, a time inside working hours for the person who
        must make decisions, or a rotating burden across a recurring series. State the rule in the
        invite. Without a rule, teams often optimise for the person who sends the first calendar
        request rather than the people who attend it.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-8">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">4. Check the next transition, then share</h2>
      <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-paper-700">
        Test the selected hour today and again around the next daylight-saving change. Then share a
        link or invite description that lists all three local times and names the anchor city. This
        is more reliable than asking every attendee to do mental offset arithmetic from an acronym.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
</Base>
